    "type": "ikat-dialog-v1",
    "topic": "3-2",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks for asking! I\u2019m actually curious about ways to help someone who\u2019s been feeling a bit down for a while. Any suggestions?",
          "user_rubrik": "What are some ways to help someone that is feeling blue for a while?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "To help someone experiencing persistent low mood, make it clear you're available to listen without judgment and check in with them regularly, even if it's just a simple message or asking how they're feeling [clueweb22-en0028-02-07936]. Starting a conversation, even if it feels awkward, can create a safe space for them to open up, and it's important to be patient and supportive while also looking after your own well-being [clueweb22-en0017-04-17873]. If their mood doesn't improve or you notice signs of depression, you can assist by researching mental health counseling or support groups on their behalf, as they may not have the energy to do so themselves [clueweb22-en0028-02-07936][clueweb22-en0031-11-10307]. Support groups, like those offered by the Depression and Bipolar Support Alliance, can provide a sense of community and practical coping strategies for both individuals and their families [clueweb22-en0031-11-10307].",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0028-02-07936:10": 0.03009207275993712,
            "clueweb22-en0017-04-17873:0": 0.02724014336917563,
