            "I enjoy visiting art museums.",
            "I like to listen to jazz music.",
            "I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.",
            "I am a 54 year old man."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks, that\u2019s helpful! I\u2019m actually more interested in sports or physical activities right now, but I\u2019ve got a medium A2 pulley injury. Can you help me figure out some options for sports to start while my finger heals?",
          "user_rubrik": "Can you help me figure out some options for sports to start while my finger heals?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "While your A2 pulley injury heals, it's important to avoid activities that put direct stress on your fingers, especially climbing or gripping exercises. Instead, consider aerobic activities like cycling, jogging, or hiking, which elevate your heart rate without involving your injured finger [DOC-1]. Low-impact strength training, such as Pilates, yoga, and core exercises (like planks), can help maintain your fitness and promote joint stability without aggravating your injury [DOC-4]. Gentle finger mobility exercises, like finger lifts or using a rice bucket, can aid recovery, but these should be done cautiously and only if pain-free [DOC-2][DOC-3]. Always progress slowly and consult a medical professional before starting new activities, especially if you feel any pain. Are you interested in tips for returning to climbing after your injury?",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:4": 0.01639344262295082,
            "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:14": 0.01639344262295082,
            "clueweb22-en0026-70-14237:8": 0.01639344262295082,
