            "Many years ago I had a bad fall while bouldering.",
            "I work at a desk job.",
            "I climb outdoors."
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Maybe later! Actually, I was wondering: is it true that cold water is not good for recovery, and I thus should not do swimming while recovering from my finger injury?",
          "user_rubrik": "Is it true that cold water is not good for recovery, and I thus should not do swimming while recovering from my finger injury?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Swimming is widely recommended as a low-impact, joint-friendly activity that can help maintain fitness during injury recovery, including tendon and finger injuries, as long as it does not cause pain or stress to the injured area [DOC-3][DOC-4]. The NSW Institute of Sport notes that cool water can actually aid in pain relief and inflammation, making it beneficial for recovery [DOC-2]. Hydrotherapy, including cold water immersion, is sometimes used as part of recovery protocols, though the optimal temperature and duration are still being studied [DOC-1]. The key is to listen to your body and avoid any activity that causes discomfort in your injured finger. If swimming does not aggravate your injury, it can be a good option, regardless of water temperature, but always consult your healthcare provider for personalized advice. Are you interested in other water-based recovery exercises?",
          "citations": {
