          "text": "Swimming is generally considered a low-impact activity, but a jammed or healing finger can make swimming uncomfortable, and repetitive motion or accidental bumps may delay healing or worsen the injury [DOC-1]. While cold water isn't specifically harmful to recovery, it can make it harder to stay warm and may reduce your swimming skills if you're already dealing with an injury [DOC-2]. For minor sprains or strains, swimming is not strictly prohibited, but you should be cautious, avoid overusing the injured finger, and consider using a waterproof bandage or splint if you do swim [DOC-2]. If you experience pain or swelling, it's best to rest and allow your finger more time to heal before returning to swimming [DOC-3]. Do you want advice on protecting your finger during daily activities?",
          "citations": {
