          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks for the guidance! I'd love to know more about swimming strokes that are finger-friendly. What would you suggest?",
          "user_rubrik": "Do you know of any water spot near London to do swimming?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 0,
          "text": "For a healing finger, the most finger-friendly swimming strokes are:\n\n**Backstroke:**  \n- Highly recommended for injury recovery. It minimizes pressure on the hands and fingers, as your palms remain relaxed and open. This stroke is especially good if you want to avoid gripping or forceful hand movements.\n\n**Gentle Freestyle (Front Crawl):**  \n- Can be safe if you keep your hand relaxed and avoid strong pulls or tight finger closure. Focus on smooth, easy movements and avoid sprinting.\n\n**Dog Paddle:**  \n- Simple and effective for staying active in the water without stressing your fingers. No forceful hand use required.\n\n**What to Avoid:**  \n- Breaststroke and butterfly, as both require strong finger extension and forceful hand movements, which could strain your healing finger.\n\nGiven your climbing background and focus on recovery, backstroke is the safest and most restorative option. Would you like tips on adapting your swim technique or suggestions for water-based rehab exercises?",
