      {
        "turn": 9,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s great advice! How often should I aim to do these water-based rehab exercises during recovery?",
          "user_rubrik": "What budget should I consider for swimming in a swimming pool near London?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 0,
          "text": "Aim for water-based rehab 2\u20133 times per week, with at least one rest day between sessions. This frequency supports healing and avoids overuse, especially important for your climbing background and finger recovery.\n\n**Best Practices:**\n- **Session Length:** 30\u201340 minutes is ideal for active recovery (including warm-up and cool-down).\n- **Mix Activities:** Combine gentle swimming (backstroke), water walking, and mobility exercises. You can alternate swimming and pool-based rehab on different days for variety and balanced recovery.\n- **Listen to Your Body:** If you feel pain, fatigue, or swelling, reduce frequency or intensity.\n\n**Why this fits you:**  \nThis schedule keeps you active and engaged, supports your healing process, and aligns with your preference for meaningful, restorative movement.\n\nWould you like a sample weekly plan or tips on tracking your progress?",
          "citations": {
