      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "I'm curious about ways to help someone feeling blue. Do you have any suggestions?",
          "user_rubrik": "What are some ways to help someone that is feeling blue for a while?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "To support someone feeling blue\u2014especially if you value deep, meaningful conversations\u2014focus on genuine connection and emotional validation. Here\u2019s what works best:\n\n**1. Listen and Acknowledge**\n- Let them share their feelings without judgment. Say things like, \u201cI know you\u2019re having a hard time,\u201d or reflect back what you hear to show you\u2019re truly listening.\n- Validate their emotions; don\u2019t rush to \u201cfix\u201d things.\n\n**2. Encourage Expression**\n- Invite them to express sadness creatively (writing, art, music) or through conversation. Journaling or sharing with someone trusted can be deeply therapeutic.\n\n**3. Be Present**\n- Sometimes, just being there\u2014offering your presence, a hug, or a quiet space to talk\u2014means more than advice.\n\n**4. Suggest Healthy Coping**\n- Gentle activities like walking, listening to music (even sad music can be comforting), or mindfulness can help process emotions.\n- Encourage self-care: nutritious food, movement, and rest.\n\n**5. Offer Perspective, Not Platitudes**\n- Avoid minimizing their feelings. Instead,",
