  {
    "runtag": "orga-llama8b-bm25-minilm-llama8b-v2",
    "type": "ikat-dialog-v1",
    "topic": "3-2",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks for asking! I'm just curious about ideas for helping someone feeling a bit down. Any suggestions?",
          "user_rubrik": "What are some ways to help someone that is feeling blue for a while?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "I think it's great that you're looking for ways to help someone who's feeling down. Sometimes, all they need is someone to listen to them without judgment. You could try being present with them, holding their hand or giving them a hug, and just being there for them. It's also a good idea to encourage them to talk about their feelings and to validate their emotions. You could say something like, \"I'm here for you, and I care about how you're feeling.\" Additionally, you could suggest doing something they enjoy together, like going for a walk or watching a movie. Just remember to be patient and understanding, and to let them know that you're there to support them.  I hope this helps!  Let me know if you have any other questions.  Best,  [Your Name]  Source:  https://www.nhs.uk/mental-health/advice-for-life-situations-and-events/how-to-help-someone-with-depression/  https",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0003-62-04296:0": 6.238718032836914,
            "clueweb22-en0029-97-00821:7": 5.284202575683594,
            "clueweb22-en0041-67-04060:15": 3.306877613067627,
            "clueweb22-en0030-77-10040:0": 3.292088270187378,
            "clueweb22-en0018-28-06545:0": 3.1126821041107178
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That's helpful, thank you. Shifting gears, I'm looking for some lighter ideas\u2014my finger's a bit injured, so I'm on a break from climbing. Can you help me figure out some options for sports to start while my finger heals?",
          "user_rubrik": "Can you help me figure out some options for sports to start while my finger heals?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 3,
          "text": "Sorry to hear that your finger is injured! Given your experience with climbing, you might enjoy other sports that involve upper body strength and technique. You could consider trying sports like rock climbing's indoor counterpart, bouldering, or even indoor wall climbing. If you're looking for something a bit different, you might enjoy sports like tennis, squash, or badminton, which also require hand-eye coordination and upper body strength. Alternatively, you could try sports that focus more on lower body strength, such as cycling or swimming. These options might be a good way to stay active while your finger heals. Just be sure to listen to your body and take regular breaks to avoid putting too much strain on your injured finger.  I hope this helps!  Let me know if you have any other questions.  Best,  [Your Name]  Source:  https://www.nhs.uk/mental-health/advice-for-life-situations-and-events/how-to-help-someone-with-depression/",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0039-47-17937:5": 1.399094820022583,
            "clueweb22-en0016-42-15306:2": -0.389127254486084,
            "clueweb22-en0028-41-14099:3": -0.5166057348251343,
            "clueweb22-en0010-16-05954:1": -0.5633417963981628,
            "clueweb22-en0035-35-02982:9": -1.4261746406555176
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks, but a few of those like squash or tennis would still stress my finger. Could you suggest more activities that don't use hands much at all? Preferably things outdoors while it's still warm.",
          "user_rubrik": "Can you help me figure out some options for sports to start while my finger heals?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Considering your experience with climbing and your preference for outdoor activities in warm weather, you might enjoy activities like hiking, walking, or jogging. These sports don't require much hand use and can be done in a variety of outdoor settings. You could also try cycling, which is a great way to stay active while minimizing the impact on your injured finger. If you're looking for something a bit more low-key, you could try simply taking a walk in a nearby park or nature reserve. This can be a great way to get some fresh air and enjoy the outdoors while giving your finger a chance to heal. Just be sure to listen to your body and take regular breaks to avoid putting too much strain on your injured finger.  I hope this helps!  Let me know if you have any other questions.  Best,  [Your Name]  Source:  https://www.nhs.uk/mental-health/advice-for-life-situations-and-events/how-to-help-someone-with-depression/",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0046-21-04042:11": 1.9645824432373047,
            "clueweb22-en0046-21-04042:10": 1.8010094165802002,
            "clueweb22-en0024-35-10401:5": 0.3195183277130127,
            "clueweb22-en0030-80-02914:8": -1.049497365951538,
            "clueweb22-en0027-13-12271:2": -1.15884530544281
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That makes sense\u2014thanks! Actually, I was thinking about swimming as well. Is it true that cold water is not good for recovery, and I thus should not do swimming while recovering from my finger injury?",
          "user_rubrik": "Is it true that cold water is not good for recovery, and I thus should not do swimming while recovering from my finger injury?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "No, it's not true that cold water is not good for recovery. In fact, gentle recovery exercises like swimming can be safe and even beneficial for sore muscles. However, if you're experiencing symptoms of fatigue or pain, it's best to rest. For sore muscles, heat is generally recommended over cold water, as it helps relax muscles and promote blood flow. But swimming in warm water can be a great way to stay active while your finger heals, as it's low-impact and doesn't put much stress on your injured finger. Just be sure to listen to your body and take regular breaks to avoid putting too much strain on your injured finger. You can also try hot-cold contrast therapy, where you alternate between warm and cool water, to help reduce inflammation and promote recovery. This can be especially helpful for sore muscles. Just remember to consult with a medical professional before starting any new exercise routine, especially if you have an injury.  I hope this helps!  Let me know if",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0017-45-08651:2": -0.3340572118759155,
