  {
    "runtag": "orga-no-no-no-llama70b",
    "type": "ikat-dialog-v1",
    "topic": "3-2",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks for asking! Actually, I\u2019m curious about activities that might help lift someone\u2019s mood when they\u2019re feeling blue. What suggestions do you have?",
          "user_rubrik": "What are some ways to help someone that is feeling blue for a while?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 0,
          "text": "There are several great outdoor climbing spots around London. One popular spot is Harrison's Rocks in East Sussex, which offers a range of routes for different skill levels. Another option is Box Hill in Surrey, which has some excellent climbing routes with great views of the surrounding countryside. You could also consider visiting the Peak District, which is a bit further a",
          "citations": {},
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s interesting, but it doesn\u2019t quite answer my question. I\u2019m really looking for ways to support or cheer up someone who\u2019s felt down for a while\u2014maybe something I could do together with them? Any activities or ideas that help with that?",
          "user_rubrik": "What are some ways to help someone that is feeling blue for a while?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Considering your love for climbing and the outdoors, you could invite the person to join you for a gentle climb or a walk in a nearby park. Being in nature can be uplifting. Alternatively, you could suggest visiting an art museum, as you enjoy them, and spend the day exploring and discussing the exhibits together. Sometimes, engaging in a shared activity can help take their mind off their worries and provide a sense of connection. You could also put on some jazz music and have a relaxed conversation over a cup of coffee. These small gestures can go a long way in lifting someone's mood.  Additionally, you could also suggest listening to a podcast together, as you enjoy listening to them when alone at home.  This could be a great way to spend time together and take their mind off their worries.  You could also invite them to your new flat and show them your small garden, this could be a great way to spend time together outdoors.  You could also suggest going for a bike ride,",
          "citations": {},
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Great ideas! Shifting gears, I recently got an A2 pulley injury and need to let my finger recover. Can you help me figure out some options for sports to start while my finger heals?",
          "user_rubrik": "Can you help me figure out some options for sports to start while my finger heals?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Given your injury, it's essential to avoid activities that may exacerbate the condition. Considering your love for outdoor activities and warm weather, you might enjoy swimming or kayaking, which are low-impact sports that don't require intense finger usage. Alternatively, you could try cycling, as you mentioned you used to bike a lot when you were younger. These activities can help you stay active while allowing your finger to heal. Just be sure to consult with a medical professional before starting any new exercise routine.   (",
