    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks for asking! I\u2019m actually more curious about how to help others, especially if they've been feeling low for a while. Any ideas?",
          "user_rubrik": "What are some ways to help someone that is feeling blue for a while?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "great that you want to support them. practical things that help : - check in regularly and listen without fixing or judging. use open questions like \u201c what \u2019 s been hardest lately? \u201d validate ( \u201c that sounds really tough \u201d ) rather than comparing to your own experiences. - offer specific, doable help : cook a meal, do laundry or shopping, drive them to appointments, or watch their kids so they can rest. - help them get support : offer to book a gp appointment with them, go along or wait outside, and help them note what they want to say. you can also look up local peer or parenting groups together. - encourage small, manageable routines : a short walk, shared coffee, or a low \u2011 key outing. follow their pace. - respect boundaries, keep confidences ( unless safety is at risk ), and be patient \u2014 low mood can take time to lift. if you \u2019 re worried about their safety, ask directly if they \u2019 re thinking about suicide. if they are ( or you \u2019 re unsure ), don \u2019 t leave them alone \u2014 contact their gp, call samaritans ( 116 123, 24 / 7 ), nhs 111, or 999 in an emergency. also, look after",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0032-70-01792:6": 3.922301769256592,
            "clueweb22-en0032-70-01792:5": 1.5452547073364258,
            "clueweb22-en0020-84-00763:15": 0.6127263903617859,
            "clueweb22-en0024-55-01699:0": 0.1412980705499649,
            "clueweb22-en0000-40-15940:0": -0.1488095372915268
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That's super helpful, thanks. Changing gears a bit\u2014I\u2019m looking for some new physical activities to try that won\u2019t bother a healing finger. Can you help me figure out some options for sports to start while my finger heals?",
          "user_rubrik": "Can you help me figure out some options for sports to start while my finger heals?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "absolutely. aim for activities that keep load off the fingers and avoid strong gripping or open \u2011 hand weight bearing. great options - brisk walking or easy hiking ; if you use poles, rely on wrist straps and a loose grip. - stationary or recumbent cycling ; outdoors only if braking / shifting is pain \u2011 free. padded half \u2011 finger cycling gloves can reduce bar pressure. - jogging or power walking intervals. - water workouts : aqua jogging or water walking ; gentle lap swim if you can keep the hand relaxed ( skip paddles and kickboards ). - elliptical using the fixed handles. - lower \u2011 body and core strength : squats, lunges, step \u2011 ups, glute bridges, calf raises, dead bugs, side planks / forearm planks. - mobility / mind \u2013 body : tai chi, qigong, yoga with modifications ( use fists, blocks, or forearms ; avoid poses loading fingers ). - climbing \u201c antagonist \u201d and shoulder work : scapular pulls, rotator \u2011 cuff band work, forearm extensor work with rubber bands. protect / modify - buddy tape or a light splint if advised. - for warm \u2011 weather sun and comfort, lightweight upf fingerless gloves ; for biking",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0021-16-02288:0": -0.25817105174064636,
            "clueweb22-en0007-21-01502:0": -0.5418435335159302,
            "clueweb22-en0030-72-04076:0": -0.6197707056999207,
            "clueweb22-en0030-72-04076:1": -0.6308964490890503,
            "clueweb22-en0004-52-01088:0": -0.6748385429382324
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks, that\u2019s useful. Quick one\u2014heard mixed things about swimming in cold water while recovering from finger injuries. Is it bad for recovery, or should I just avoid swimming till it\u2019s better?",
          "user_rubrik": "Is it true that cold water is not good for recovery, and I thus should not do swimming while recovering from my finger injury?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "short answer : you don \u2019 t have to avoid swimming entirely, but be smart about temperature and load. - acute phase ( first 1 \u2013 2 weeks or if pain / swelling persists ) : skip cold - water swims. cold constricts blood flow and can mask pain, which may slow healing or lead to re \u2011 injury. prefer gentle movement in neutral \u2013 warm water, or do aqua jogging instead. - after the acute phase : easy pool swimming is fine if the hand stays relaxed. avoid paddles, hard pull sets, kickboards ( they require gripping ), and sprinting. try side - kick or streamline kicking without a board, vertical kicking, or aqua jogging. - cold open water : if you choose to go, keep dips short, warm up on land first, wear neoprene swim gloves, stop before your hands feel numb, and rewarm immediately. if pain or swelling increases later that day or next, scale back. - avoid any swimming if you have open cuts, a splint that shouldn \u2019 t get wet, or a suspected pulley / ligament tear \u2014 get clearance first. given you prefer warm weather, waiting for warmer water or sticking to a heated pool is the safer bet",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0001-58-13690:0": -0.35110682249069214,
            "clueweb22-en0002-70-06023:0": -1.5503326654434204,
            "clueweb22-en0016-53-02027:0": -2.2760024070739746,
            "clueweb22-en0004-35-16961:4": -3.880643844604492,
            "clueweb22-en0038-43-07732:6": -4.124289512634277
          },
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 4,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks, that clears things up. Do you know of any water spot near London to do swimming?",
          "user_rubrik": "Do you know of any water spot near London to do swimming?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Absolutely\u2014here are good, finger\u2011friendly swim options in/near London:  - London Fields Lido (Hackney): 50m outdoor pool, heated year\u2011round. Great for steady laps; bookable lane/casual sessions. - Charlton Lido (SE7): 50m heated outdoor pool with family sessions; reliable temperature for an easy, relaxed hand. - Hampton Pool (Hampton/TW12): Heated open\u2011air pool with a family vibe and grass areas; nice on warm days. - Brockwell Lido (Herne Hill): Iconic but unheated\u2014best only on hot days if you want to avoid cold stress on the finger. - Parliament Hill Lido (Hampstead Heath): Unheated; go in summer for gentler conditions. - Beckenham Place Park Swimming Lake: Lifeguarded open\u2011water sessions; consider a wetsuit if the water\u2019s cool.  Tips for your healing finger: - Prefer heated pools and keep the hand relaxed; skip paddles, kickboards, and sprints. - Book ahead and check each venue\u2019s session type/temperature. - Aim for midday slots for warmer water; re\u2011warm hands quickly after any cooler swim.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0028-41-15939:10": -0.9431380033493042,
            "clueweb22-en0008-69-05905:12": -1.7103936672210693,
            "clueweb22-en0028-89-17864:4": -2.0662381649017334,
            "clueweb22-en0023-06-14225:1": -3.99202823638916,
            "clueweb22-en0023-06-14225:0": -4.073060512542725
          },
