    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Thanks for asking! I\u2019m curious, what are some interesting ways to help someone who\u2019s been feeling down for a bit?",
          "user_rubrik": "What are some ways to help someone that is feeling blue for a while?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Here are some gentle, interesting ways to support someone who\u2019s been down:  - Offer a low-pressure writing option: give them a notebook and suggest free-writing, short daily notes, or a poem\u2014no need for \u201cgood\u201d words, just honest thoughts.   - Create a shared ritual: invite them to write with you for 10\u201315 minutes, then talk or sit quietly\u2014company lowers the bar.   - Try creative journaling: doodling, hand-lettering a comforting quote, or decorating a favorite verse can be calming and meditative.   - Encourage gentle movement: a body-positive yoga class or a short, guided stretch walk with modifications and props can help mood without pressure.   - Small practical comforts: make tea, cook a simple meal, go for a short walk, or sit and listen\u2014validation matters more than fixing.   - If they\u2019re struggling long-term, gently suggest professional help and offer to assist with finding resources or going with them.  Ask them what feels most helpful\u2014letting them choose empowers recovery.",
          "citations": {
            "clueweb22-en0045-47-11540:18": 19.1875,
