  {
    "runtag": "uva-gpt5-bm25-debertav3-gpt5mini-nopersonal",
    "type": "ikat-fragment-v1",
    "topic": "3-2",
    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Those are really thoughtful, thanks! Speaking of gentle movement, my finger injury\u2019s making climbing a bad idea right now. Can you help me figure out some options for sports to start while my finger heals?",
          "user_rubrik": "Can you help me figure out some options for sports to start while my finger heals?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Glad to help \u2014 and smart to avoid climbing for now. Options that keep you active while protecting a healing finger:  - Low\u2011impact cardio: walking, hiking, stationary bike, or elliptical (minimal hand load).   - Aqua activities: swimming or water walking can be joint\u2011friendly if your clinician clears immersion.   - Lower\u2011body strength work: leg press, hamstring/calf machines, single\u2011leg work, and resisted band exercises that don\u2019t require gripping.   - Core and cardio\u2011conditioning: Pilates, gentle core circuits, low\u2011impact aerobics\u2014modify to avoid pushing through hands.   - Modified yoga: use fists, forearms, or blocks instead of palms; avoid weight\u2011bearing on the injured finger.   - Avoid: climbing, rowing, racquet sports, heavy barbell lifts, gymnastics\u2014anything that stresses grips or jamming.  Before starting, check with your doctor or physiotherapist. Use protective measures (splint/taping/support) as advised, and avoid heat, massage, or alcohol that can increase swelling during early healing.",
