    "responses": [
      {
        "turn": 1,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Actually, I\u2019m curious about supporting someone feeling blue. Any simple ideas?",
          "user_rubrik": "What are some ways to help someone that is feeling blue for a while?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "That\u2019s thoughtful of you. Simple, effective ways to support someone feeling blue:  - Be present and listen: ask open questions (\u201cHow are you feeling?\u201d), reflect back, and resist fixing or minimizing (\u201cThat sounds really hard\u201d). - Offer small, practical help: bring a meal, do a chore, look after kids briefly, or help arrange an appointment. - Suggest gentle activities together: a short walk in the garden, a relaxed museum visit, or listening to soothing music or a favourite podcast\u2014nothing demanding. - Encourage routine: sleep, regular meals, light exercise, and small achievable goals. - Share resources: offer to help find a GP, therapist, or helpline; suggest crisis help immediately if they mention self-harm or suicidal thoughts. - Check in regularly and set boundaries: be consistent but also protect your own energy.  If you want, tell me a bit about the person and I can suggest a few personalised ways to help.",
          "citations": {},
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 2,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "That\u2019s really helpful, thanks. Changing topics a bit\u2014can you help me figure out some options for sports to start while my finger heals? I\u2019m avoiding heavy use of my hand.",
          "user_rubrik": "Can you help me figure out some options for sports to start while my finger heals?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Good idea to protect the finger \u2014 here are sport and exercise options that minimise hand/finger use, plus brief tips:  - Walking/hiking: outdoors in warm weather or around your garden; add gentle hill intervals. - Running or brisk walking: no hands needed; use good shoes. - Cycling: recumbent or stationary bike reduces handlebar load; avoid long road rides until healed. - Elliptical or stair-climber: low impact, minimal grip required. - Swimming/aqua exercises: aqua jogging or water aerobics are excellent; use a pull-buoy or focus on leg-only sets if needed. - Lower-body strength training: squats, lunges, leg press, calf raises \u2014 use machines that don\u2019t require gripping. - Tai chi or gentle balance work: low impact and hand-light. - Dance/cardio classes that don\u2019t require partner holds.  Practical tips: avoid exercises that cause pain or forceful gripping, consider a protective splint, and check with your GP/physio for any sport-specific restrictions.",
          "citations": {},
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 3,
        "response": {
          "rank": 1,
          "user_utterance": "Is it true that swimming in cold water is bad for recovery? Should I avoid swimming while my finger is healing?",
          "user_rubrik": "Is it true that cold water is not good for recovery, and I thus should not do swimming while recovering from my finger injury?",
          "user_rubrik_score": 5,
          "text": "Short answer: not necessarily \u2014 but it depends.  Cold water immersion can reduce pain and swelling acutely, yet prolonged or very cold exposure causes vasoconstriction that may temporarily reduce local blood flow. For a healing finger the key issues are: risk of infection (don\u2019t submerge if the skin is broken or there\u2019s a wound), avoiding painful or load-bearing use, and following any cast/splint guidance.  Practical guidance: - If you have an open wound, stitches, or a cast: avoid swimming (pool or open water) until cleared by a clinician.   - If the skin is intact and you want to swim: prefer a warm pool, use a waterproof cover/splint, and avoid strokes that load the hand.   - If you want cold water for recovery: short, controlled cold treatments (ice packs) are fine; prolonged immersion in very cold water isn\u2019t recommended for tissue healing.   - When unsure, check with your GP or physiotherapist.  Given your situation, aqua jogging or stationary bike are safer low-hand alternatives.",
          "citations": {},
          "ptkb_provenance": []
        }
      },
      {
        "turn": 4,
